ANALYSIS:
- The rule accesses PipelineRun attestations through `lib.pipelinerun_attestations` and iterates over each attestation to check for tasks
- The rule checks if `tekton.tasks(att)` returns an empty set (count == 0) for any attestation, which would indicate no tasks were found
- The core validation logic triggers a violation when no tasks are present in any of the PipelineRun attestations, causing the deny rule to fire
- The result is generated using `lib.result_helper` with the rule metadata and an empty array, producing a failure message "No tasks found in PipelineRun attestation"

RULE:
```rego
package tasks

import rego.v1
import data.lib
import data.lib.json as j
import data.lib.tekton

# METADATA
# title: Pipeline run includes at least one task
# description: >-
#   Ensure that at least one Task is present in the PipelineRun
#   attestation.
# custom:
#   short_name: pipeline_has_tasks
#   failure_msg: No tasks found in PipelineRun attestation
#   solution: >-
#     Make sure the build pipeline ran any tasks and that the build system is
#     generating a proper attestation.
#   collections:
#   - minimal
#   - redhat
#   - redhat_rpms
#   - slsa3
#   depends_on:
#   - attestation_type.known_attestation_type
#
deny contains result if {
	some att in lib.pipelinerun_attestations
	count(tekton.tasks(att)) == 0
	result := lib.result_helper(rego.metadata.chain(), [])
}
```

