ANALYSIS:
- The rule accesses CycloneDX SBOM data through `sbom.cyclonedx_sboms` and checks package URLs (purls) against disallowed packages defined in rule data
- The rule iterates over all SBOMs and their components, checking each component's purl against the list of disallowed packages using `sbom.has_item()` function
- The core validation logic triggers a violation when a component's purl is found in the disallowed packages list, causing the deny rule to return a result
- The result is generated using `lib.result_helper()` which creates a structured failure message with the disallowed package name and metadata from the rule chain

RULE:
```rego
package sbom_cyclonedx

import rego.v1
import data.lib
import data.lib.sbom

# METADATA
# title: Allowed
# description: >-
#   Confirm the CycloneDX SBOM contains only allowed packages. By default all packages are allowed.
#   Use the "disallowed_packages" rule data key to provide a list of disallowed packages.
# custom:
#   short_name: allowed
#   failure_msg: "Package is not allowed: %s"
#   solution: >-
#     Update the image to not use any disallowed package.
#   collections:
#   - redhat
#   - redhat_rpms
#
deny contains result if {
	some s in sbom.cyclonedx_sboms
	some component in s.components
	sbom.has_item(component.purl, lib.rule_data(sbom.rule_data_packages_key))
	result := lib.result_helper(rego.metadata.chain(), [component.purl])
}
```

